n effects of factors A, C, and D,\n- \\( \\beta_{AC}, \\beta_{AD}, \\beta_{CD} \\) are the interaction effects,\n- \\( \\epsilon \\) represents the random error.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**  \nThe design layout for the Pilot Plant Filtration Rate Experiment is structured as a factorial design, which can be either a full factorial or a fractional factorial design depending on the number of levels and factors involved. Each combination of factor levels is tested to assess its impact on the filtration rate.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**  \nThe validity of the results from the Pilot Plant Filtration Rate Experiment relies on several key assumptions:\n1. Independence of observations: Each experimental run should be independent of others.\n2. Normality: The residuals should be normally distributed.\n3. Homogeneity of variance: The variance of the response variable should be constant across all levels of the factors.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nThis experiment is applicable when the researcher aims to understand the effects of multiple factors on a response variable simultaneously. It is particularly useful when:\n- The factors are believed to interact,\n- The number of factors is manageable,\n- The goal is to optimize the filtration process.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis of the data collected from the Pilot Plant Filtration Rate Experiment typically involves:\n1. Conducting an Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) to determine the significance of the main effects and interactions.\n2. If significant effects are found, post-hoc tests or further analysis may be conducted to explore differences between factor levels.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \nThe factorial design offers several advantages:\n- It allows for the assessment of interaction effects between factors, which can provide deeper insights into the filtration process.\n- It is efficient in terms of resource utilization, as multiple factors are tested simultaneou